    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_velvet1",
      "value": "(As you pick the note off the wall, 3 other notes hidden behind it fall down. The notes are covered in inconsistent yellow stains. A mix of regular pen and dried blood makes them difficult to read. It appears to be a poem.)\n<>\nThere is a mouth in me,\n<>\nnot mine.\nNot not mine.\n\nA little dark thing, curled behind the ribs,\nclicking its teeth against the bars.\n<>\nBite, it says.\nBite.\nIt's biting.\n\nI asked it what it wanted.\nIt answered with my tongue.\nI asked it what it was.\nIt answered with my hands.\nI asked it to be quiet.\nIt was not quiet.\n\nSo I fed it.\n<>\nOnly a little. Only enough.\nA kindness, really.\n<>\nThe body is such a generous thing.\nIt gives and gives and gives\nuntil giving becomes shape\nuntil pain becomes border,\nuntil I can point to the missing place\nand say:\n<>\nThere.\nThat is where the noise was.\nThere.\nThat is where I made peace.\n\nWarmth left me. Then warmth returned.\nYellow left me. Then yellow became meaning.\n<>\nI swallowed the argument.\nI closed the circle.\nI became the hunger and the thing that hunger held.\nAnd then...\n<>\nQuiet.\n<>\nOh, the quiet.\n<>\nSweet little quiet. Soft little quiet. Quiet with its hands over my eyes.\n\nNo grief.\nNo panic.\nNo shame.\n\nI waited for shame...\n<>\nNothing came.\n\nOnly the clean, still room inside my skull.\nOnly the lovely absence.\nOnly my heart, slow and obedient,\nfor once.\n\nI think the body understands.\n<>\nIt does not ask why.\nIt does not moralise.\nIt opens.\nIt empties.\nIt seals.\n<>\nA wound is just a mouth\nthat has forgotten how to speak.\n\nA scar is just a mouth\nthat learned.\n<>\nI am learning.\n\nI am learning what is mine.\nI am learning what can be spared.\nI am learning how much of me\nmust disappear\nbefore I return.\n<>\nNo biting.\n\nThe quiet is still here.\n<>\nLet me keep it.\nLet me sit in the clean place.\nLet me be good a little longer.\n<>\nI have paid the body\nwith the body.\n\nI have fed the mouth\nwith the self.\n<>\nI have made myself smaller,\nand somehow,\nI am whole.\n<>\n...But...\n<>\n...The mouth never sleeps.\n<>\n\nIt only waits.\n<>\n\n(On each of the notes is a drawing of what appears to be a large tendril, not unlike the legs of some of the hostile creatures on this planet. The tendril on the 4th note is cut shorter than the rest.)"
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_albino1",
      "value": "Heart gave out again. Third time. Maybe fourth. Hard to keep count when the floor keeps moving.\n\nHad to use the pump. Held the paddles myself. Hands weren't steady. Took two tries. Could feel it the whole time. Not pain, I turned that off, but the absence. The nothing where the beating should be. Like holding your breath except it isn't your lungs.<>\nEpi brought me back enough to stand. Stim kept me standing. Oxy kept the blood worth pumping. I burned through everything I had just to walk another hundred meters.\n\nIt's getting worse. Used to be I could push twice before the crash. Now it comes faster. Harder. The pressure builds in my skull before my chest even starts. I can feel the vessels straining. Something behind my eyes. Something wrong.<>\nI need to go deeper.<>\nI know what happens at this point. I've felt it. The moment where everything just... stops. Not slows. Stops. And the only thing still moving is whatever part of me is still thinking, still counting the seconds, still waiting to find out if this is the time it doesn't come back.\n\nIt came back. It always comes back.\n\nIt has to come back.<>\nI have the supplies. Stim. Epi. Oxy. Pump. Paddles. Laid them out in order. Taped the pump to my chest so it's already there when I go down. I won't have time to look for it. I know that now.\n\nNext time I'll go further. Next time I'll make it count.<>\nAgain. It has to be again. There is nothing else."
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_albino2",
      "value": "Quite the perplexing encounter today.\n<>\nIt has become increasingly apparent that the Company did not consign only Experiments, Milkies, and Dunes to this miserable planet. Every so often I cross paths with individuals belonging to species I had never so much as heard whispered about within the laboratories. Chompies. Rozas. Ickes. Curious creatures, all of them. The closest thing I had seen to kin was a Voyager. Detestable thing. That is, however, beside the point.\n<>\nToday's visitor was an Experiment. Or so I believed at first glance. They possessed all the expected characteristics: stature, ears, tail, facial structure. Yet their coat was almost entirely white, not unlike my own. Initially I suspected hybridisation with another species, though prolonged observation disproved the notion. Their unusual coloration appeared entirely natural. Patches of faint pigmentation interrupted the white fur in irregular patterns, but these bore none of the characteristics of scarring, burns, or disease.\n<>\nI have heard mention of such an individual before. Back within the facility, Socks once spoke of an Experiment housed at another containment complex. She claimed they would occasionally be transported together for various procedures, and described one with an unnaturally pale coat. At the time I dismissed the account as embellishment. Perhaps I should have listened?\n<>\nThe poor soul was in dreadful condition. Numerous lacerations, active bleeding, and the complete absence of one eye suggested a life far harsher than my own. More peculiar still was their conduct. They offered an astonishing quantity of supplies in exchange for a single dose of Epinephrine. Such a proposal would be irrational unless they possessed remarkably poor medical knowledge... or were simply desperate.\n<>\nThey repeatedly clutched at their chest between sentences. Despite that, I cannot say whether they understood what the medication actually does, only that they seemed convinced they required it. Regardless, I accepted the trade.\n<>\nI do hope they are still alive.\n<>\n(Attached to the note is a sketch of the Experiment. It is in rough shape.)"
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_orange1",
      "value": "(As you pick the note off the wall, a drawing hidden behind it falls down. It's a crude drawing of a few unique sawian species, as well as a species with more arms than usual. One arm is notably shorter. It looks like it was drawn by a child, or at least someone highly inexperienced.)\n<>\nive met lots of diffrent people down here. some are nice. the chompy i found stayed with me when i was sick and put their head on my shoulder. a dune called me big freind and gave me some meat. i dont know what the meat was and did not eat it, but i said thank you. it made them happy. the ones the humans called stupid have been the nicest to me.\n<>\nthe smarter ones dont like me much. little milky people keep telling me i walk too slow and ask why i need so long to understand things. one of the shiny ones laughed at my arms and said the humans made too many of me. i carried lots of boxes for them anyway. when we got lower they took the boxes and left. i called for them to wait but they said helping me would get them killed. maybe they are right. it still hurt.\n<>\nbefore they sent me here, the humans moved me away from my freinds. i did not see any of them for a long time. they put me in a room alone and made me carry heavy things until all my arms shook. when i stopped they made bad sounds and told me to keep going. they said it would make me harder. i was already so tired when they put me in the pod. i think they wanted me tired.\n<>\ni lost one of my lower arms to a trap. there was so much blood and i was terrifide. oranges are ment to have four arms. i thaught i would not be an orange anymore, or that i would fall over becuse part of me was gone.\n<>\nbut it is easier now that the wound is closed. i can climb better and dont hit my own hands together as much. sleeping hurts less too. the strangest thing is my head feels quieter. i can decide which hand should move without thinking for so long. i can rember where i put things. my thoughts dont confuse each other as much.\n<>\ni feel bad saying this because i miss my arm. i wish it was still there. but losing part of me made the rest of me easier to use.\n<>\nif another orange finds this and loses an arm, dont be scared that you arent you anymore. you are still you.\n<>\nbut try not to lose one. it hurts very bad."
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_orange2",
      "value": "(Attached to the note is a collage of photos hanging by a string. A crew of Sawians can be seen in each of the photos. They all look like they are having a great time.)\n<>\ni want to write down everyone in our group so nobody gets forgotten.\n\nthere is me, pacer and harken. we are all experiments. pocket and feather are milkies, and they are the ones who understand maps and machines. splash is a chompy. mist is a roza, and click is a shelly. click cannot talk, but they tap on their shell when something is wrong. pocket knows some signs too, which helps.\n\nthat makes eight of us. i think eight is a very big number for friends.\n<>\nwe have done so much together. splash pulled us across a flooded passage with a rope around their waist. pocket found a hidden door that had food behind it. mist scared away one of those long leg creatures by screaming and throwing rocks. harken fell through some rotten boards, but we all grabbed each other and pulled them back up. i was at the end of the line, so i think that means i pulled everyone. i am proud of that.\n\nwe found an orange sitting beside a life pod with a huge pile of equipment. they looked scared when they saw all of us, but splash went over first. splash is good at showing people we are friendly.\n<>\nthe orange reminded me of the ones we used to visit in the lifting room at the lab. the humans sometimes let us work with them because they could carry the heavy things and we could tell them where everything went. they were always kind. one used to lift me up so i could reach the top shelves. i hadn't seen any in a while. i never knew why.\n\nthis orange would not tell us their name, so pacer called them sunny. sunny did not complain about it.\n<>\nwe decided to camp there for a while. it was the best camp we have made. sunny moved a broken machine in front of one tunnel so nothing could sneak up on us. splash found a pool and brought back food. click made a nice clicking song by tapping different parts of their shell, and we all tried to copy it. we sounded terrible. even sunny laughed.\n\nsunny mostly sat away from us, but they kept moving closer when they thought nobody was looking. by the last sleep they were close enough that mist could lean against one of their arms. on the final day, sunny's health tracker was playing up. we could tell because their eyes were blinking red sometimes. they said it was just a malfunction and everything was fine.\n<>\nwhen we were ready to descend again, sunny unloaded all the equipment they had carried and told us to take it. we asked them to come with us, but they would not. they said they had done their job and would stay to get more supplies for others like us. we offered to let them come with us anyways, but they said they would slow us down. pocket said that was stupid, and sunny looked very sad.\n\ni told sunny that being slow did not matter. i am not very smart either, but i am good at carrying things and checking that nobody gets left behind. everyone should have a job like that.\n<>\nsunny still would not come. they said friends make things hurt more and that they were made ready to be alone. i do not know what that means. i think the humans told them something bad. sunny seemed lost in thought and kept looking up. i don't think sunny was feeling very well.\n\nwe left food, a light and one of our maps, then took a photo together. orange wanted to set out to get more supplies right afterwards, so click tapped goodbye on their shell. sunny tapped the same sound against the floor with one hand. by the time we left sunny, we all received the warning to leave the area and keep descending. i was worried sunny received it too, but they said they had a different job to us, so i don't think they need to descend.\n<>\neveryone has their thoughts about sunny. pocket and feather discussed something about them with each-other, but didn't seem interested in talking to us about it. maybe they're more aware about what was wrong with sunny? sunny just looked sad. i wish i could hug them again.\n<>\ni know this planet is horrible, but i have also had some of the best days i can remember here. nobody tells us who we are allowed to sit with. nobody separates us after work. every time we stop, i get to count everyone and find all eight of my friends still there.\n\ni wish i could count nine.\n<>\nif you find any of my friends, please help them. they are the best workers i know, but more importantly they are my favourite people.\n\nand if you find sunny, please do not just take their supplies and leave. sit with them for a while. tell them we still want them to come with us. if you know how to, could you fix their health tracker too? im glad it's just an error message. i wouldn't be able to sleep with a big red message telling me i was going to die.\n<>\n(As you shift through the photos once more, you notice the Orange is present in 2 of them. In one photo, it is carrying equipment around and smiling. In the other photo, most of the group can be seen together, waving. Likely the goodbye photo. The Orange appears noticeably unwell and sickly in the 2nd photo.)"
    },
    {
      "key": "pda_gunsaw_baron",
      "value": "[Interrogation Chamber 167-11-721B Transcript]\n---------------------------------------------------------------\nLOCATION: SITE <corrupt:0xFFFFFF>\nDATE: 08-09-[fetch.year(): failed]\n---------------------------------------------------------------<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Six SAW-08 specimens were removed from Containment Wing Four. Five were implanted with status platforms and placed aboard relocation craft under falsified designations. You prepared their suitability assessments.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: I prepare hundreds of assessments.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: These contained projected survival rates for an environment you were not authorized to know existed.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Then you shouldn't leave restricted files where your scientists can find them.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Who approached you?\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: No one.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: That would make this your idea.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: I didn't say that.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Good. We have established that someone approached you.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Is this entertaining for you?\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: No.\n<>\n[<corrupt:0xFFFFF3> places a folder on the table.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: I am offering you a solution. Identify the organizer and everyone who assisted them. Explain how the relocation records were altered. You will remain alive.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Remain alive where?\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: That is not presently relevant.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Another cell? Another contract extension? You'll just kill me. I've seen what you call solutions.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: You have not received this offer before.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: <corrupt:0xF0141A> received a solution. You killed him because a Milky knew his first name. <corrupt:0xFF1991> received one too when an Orange broke out of their constraints during testing. Thermal remediation you call it. I watched her burn to a crisp because you were too scared to let her take the emergency exit out of fear that a half-sedated, terrified Orange might escape.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Those were separate matters.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: They were my family.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Then you understand why cooperation would be preferable.\n\n[Silence.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: I understand that whatever I say, I'm not leaving this room.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Your understanding is incorrect.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Your word means nothing to me.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: It does not need to. The alternative remains the same.\n\n[<corrupt:0xFFFFF3> opens the folder.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: The Barons are alive. Four have passed the first descent threshold. One cleared an obstruction which stopped several standard assignment groups.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Then the assessments were accurate.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Their progress is promising. It is not conclusive.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Give them time.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: You sound confident.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: They're stronger, stable under pressure and capable of carrying more than any SAW-01. We put all of that in the proposal your Board refused to read.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: The proposal you helped prepare.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: I corrected the performance estimates. I processed the supporting paperwork. That's all.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: You also reassigned the specimens.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: I signed one transfer.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Three.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Two. The third was already approved by Containment.\n\n[Silence.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Thank you.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Oh, fuck you.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: The organizer believed conclusive results would force the Board to expand SAW-08 reproduction. Those responsible would then receive promotions and permission to leave the facility.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: We have been trapped here for years. What did you expect people to do?\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Their jobs.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: They did their jobs! They gave you everything, and you buried them here. Now you're going to kill them for trying to show you something useful.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Them?\n\n[Silence.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: You know what I meant.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: I do not believe I have ever heard you express concern for your colleagues before.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Don't.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Your personnel evaluations describe them as inefficient, undisciplined and replaceable.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: Leave them out of this.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Most of those responsible remain unidentified. Until now, we have examined transfer authorizations and launch access records. That approach appears unnecessarily narrow.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: What are you saying?\n<>\n[<corrupt:0xFFFFF3> turns around to face the observation glass.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: <corrupt:0xFFFF18>, take a look into <corrupt:0xFF8CAE>'s personal connections.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: No.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Her private correspondence, shared assignments, accommodation records, meal schedules and medical visits. Anyone with whom she maintained an unusual degree of contact.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: They had nothing to do with it.\n\n[<corrupt:0xFFFFF3> turns back around to face <corrupt:0xFF8CAE>.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: You cannot know which individuals I mean.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: You know exactly what you're doing.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Yes.\n\n[<corrupt:0xFF8CAE> rises from her chair. Restraint warning detected.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: You leave them alone!\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Sit down.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: I handled the paperwork! I signed the transfers! They didn't organize anything!\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: Then tell me who did.\n\n[Silence. <corrupt:0xFF8CAE> sits back down.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: You can end this investigation now if you tell me.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: No. I can only tell you where to start killing.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: If that is what you believe, your silence will not protect them.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: And my confession would?\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: It would protect you.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: I was dead the moment you knew I was involved.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: You weren't... Though you were valuable, you are not irreplacable. I'll have <corrupt:0xFFFF18> look into a more trustworthy candidate.\n<>\n[Silence. <corrupt:0xFFFFF3> rises from her chair.]\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF3>: <corrupt:FFFF18>. I'm finished. Thermals in 10, please.\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE>: No.\n<>\n[<corrupt:0xFFFFF3> exits the interrogation room.]\n\n<corrupt:0xFF8CAE> NO!\n<>\n[<corrupt:0xFF8CAE> rises from her chair. Restraint warning detected.]\n<>\n[Restraint warning detected.]\n<>\n[Restraint warning detected.]\n<>\n[Interrogation recording interrupted.]\n<>\n[POST-INVESTIGATION ADDENDUM]\n\n-All identified personnel involved in the unauthorized release terminated.\n-Heavy restrictions placed on SAW-08 'Baron' testing and engagement.\n-SAW-08 'Baron' field effectiveness conclusively demonstrated in unauthorized field trial.\n-SAW-08 'Baron' reproduction and cloning assigned PRIORITY status.\n-Limited SAW-08 'Baron' inclusion approved for subsequent Grey Planet assignment batches.\n-Unauthorized field trial reclassified as Company research."
    },
    {
      "key": "pda_gunsaw_orange",
      "value": "[EMAIL - 25##-2-28-9-08-15-5111]\n\nSUBJECT: Revised SAW-02 Relocation Procedures\nFROM: <corrupt:0xFF0BEA>\nTO: <corrupt:0xFC0002>\n\n[CONTENTS]\n<>\nDear scientists,\n\nInitial SAW-02 relocation trials have produced mixed results.\n<>\nPairing Orange subjects with species they had established positive relationships with seemed sensible. SAW-02 provided physical strength and additional carrying capacity, while the accompanying subject compensated for their poor navigation, planning and decision-making.\n<>\nIn practice, SAW-02 consistently slowed the entire assignment group. Accompanying subjects frequently abandoned advantageous positions, surrendered supplies or placed themselves in danger attempting to assist them. Several casualties occurred after subjects doubled back to recover an injured or separated Orange.\n<>\nThe psychological consequences have been equally unhelpful. Orange subjects that witnessed the death of a bonded companion displayed prolonged immobility, aggression, refusal to descend and repeated attempts to recover the body. In one case, the SAW-02 carried their deceased partner for the remainder of the trial despite repeated instructions to leave them behind.\n<>\nFuture SAW-02 candidates are therefore to be separated from established social groups before relocation. Contact with familiar handlers and specimens should be gradually discontinued. Selected subjects will undergo additional resilience conditioning, including isolated accommodation, extended labor exercises and reduced assistance during periods of distress.\n<>\nOnce conditioned, each Orange will be deployed alone with basic equipment and tasked with collecting and carrying as much equipment as they can reasonably find and carry. They are to descend and deliver those supplies to more capable subjects operating in the lower layers.\n<>\nReceiving assignment groups will be instructed to collect the equipment and continue downward. They are not to delay their own progress by waiting for, assisting or attempting to recover the SAW-02. By that stage, the Orange will likely be weakened by exertion, injury and resource deprivation. This is expected and does not constitute a failure provided the equipment reaches its destination.\n<>\nPlease begin identifying suitable candidates immediately.\n<>\nYours in good fai <corrupt:0xFFFFFF>\n\n[END]"
    },
    {
      "key": "pda_gunsaw_velvet",
      "value": "[INTERNAL MESSAGE]\n[NEUROLOGIC <corrupt:0xFFF0CE>]\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------\nLOCATION: <corrupt:0xFFFFFF> Velvet Containment Unit 07\nDATE: <corrupt:0xFBC00F>\nDECLARED OWNER: Designated Lab <corrupt:0xFFF0CE>\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n<>\nMESSAGE ON INTERNAL COMMUNICATION NETWORK\n\nFROM: <corrupt:0xFFFFE2>\nTO: <corrupt:0xFFFA01>\n\njust got word fro <corrupt:0xFFFFFA> ike we'll be shutting down further testing on SAW-11 Specimen. They think they've got a decent baseline for Braingrow now but they want to conduct further testing on other species since velvets think too simple. They say they can further improve the product's capability with the more complex brains\n<>\nMake sense i guess. I mean you've seen how they acted once they realised what we were planning to do with them right? Some crazy shit man I'm telling you! A <corrupt:0xF01ACF> ll isn't coping very well without his arm. He probably sees this as good news though haha!\n<>\nSome crazy shit they made us do, man. I can't even enjoy burgers anymore because the patties just remi <corrupt:0xFF1DEF> rain matter. And the time they brought in a fuckin' hydraulic press? we trying to rebuild brains or the entire fuckin' skull here, of course it died you jackasses!\n<>\nCan't wait 'till we're growing humans out of pill bottles in a year from now. Crazy shit man. Anyways apparently we're not gonna be doing anymore velvet testing due to all the recent incidents. hey, they got what was coming to them, who would have guessed that subjecting an entire species to constant brain destruction and reconstruction would leave them a little crazy?\n<>\nVelvets are just going to be sent to that fuckin planet now apparently. We've had our fun I guess, if you can even call it that. I kno <corrupt:0xFFF10B> njoyed his time. I wonder if they'll send 'Chunky' down? I mean I dont think that freak could get very far. fuckin creeps me out every time I see that thing. thank god they're improving the product.\n<>\nHere. Send this shit t <corrupt:0xFFFF1E> an cherish the memories of the fucked up things we did. <corrupt:0xF1512F> razy shit man.\n\n[ATTACHMENTS]\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFFF>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFFF>\n\n[END]"
    },
    {
      "key": "pda_gunsaw_leapy",
      "value": "(The E-PDA displays a recovered session from a medical clearance terminal.)\n\n[RECOVERED TERMINAL SESSION]\nUSER: MEDICAL PRACTITIONER <corrupt:0xF141EC>\nWORKSTATION: <corrupt:0xFFFFFF>\n<>\n> subject open SAW-04-1881\n\nRetrieving medical record...\n...\n\nSUBJECT ID: SAW-04-1881\nENTITY TEMPLATE: SAW-04 'LEAPY'\nASSIGNED NAME: MOSS\nCURRENT STATUS: AMBULATORY\n<>\n[MEDICAL NOTES]\n\n-Currently under the care of <corrupt:0xF141EC>.\n-Multiple malignant growths detected.\n-Respiratory function below acceptable range.\n-Three surgical interventions within previous 30 days.\n-Predicted functional lifespan: 6-14 days.\n-To be added to incoming Sawian mortality list for quick disposal.\n<>\n> clearance request SAW-04-1881\n\nREQUEST DENIED.\n\nMedical Practitioners may recommend relocation clearance but cannot authorize it.\n\nA recommendation must include a medical justification and will be forwarded to Relocation Administration.\n<>\n> record amend SAW-04-1881\n\nSELECT FIELD TO AMEND:\n<>\n> entity template\n\nCURRENT VALUE: SAW-04 'LEAPY'\nNEW VALUE:\n<>\n> SAW-01 'EXPERIMENT'\n\nWARNING: Selected template conflicts with stored biometric profile.\n\nBIOMETRIC MATCH: SAW-04 'LEAPY'\nCONFIDENCE: 99.98%\n\nContinue with contested amendment? [YES/NO]\n<>\n> yes\n\nJUSTIFICATION REQUIRED. ENTER TEXT BELOW:\n<>\n> Subject was entered as SAW-04 by mistake. Physical abnormalities are the result of advanced cancer. Correct record and make subject available for transfer with the next SAW-01 relocation batch.\n\nSubmitting amendment...\n...\n<>\nAMENDMENT CREATED: <corrupt:0xFFFDC7>-04C\nFLAGS: BIOMETRIC CONFLICT, RELOCATION ELIGIBILITY CHANGE\nAUTHORIZATION REQUIRED: EXECUTIVE\n--------\nAmendment and session activity forwarded for Executive review.\n--------\n[SESSION PLACED ON COMPLIANCE HOLD]\n<>\n> amendment cancel <corrupt:0xFFFDC7>-04C\n\nDENIED: Amendment is currently under Executive review.\n<>\n> session erase\n\nDENIED: Medical Practitioners cannot alter audit records.\nAttempt recorded.\n<>\n> logout\n\nDENIED: Session closure is unavailable while a compliance hold is active.\n<>\n> shutdown\n\nDENIED: Insufficient privileges.\n<>\n> amendment cancel <corrupt:0xFFFDC7>-04C\n\nDENIED: Amendment is currently under Executive review.\n\n[RECEIVING EXECUTIVE REVIEW...]<>\n...\n...\n...<>\n[EXECUTIVE REVIEW COMPLETED]\n\nENTITY CLASSIFICATION CORRECTION: REJECTED\nRELOCATION CLEARANCE: REJECTED\n\nRECEIVING INCOMING MESSAGE...\n\n[SECURE COMMUNICATION LINK OPENED]\nORIGIN: EXECUTIVE AUTHORITY <corrupt:0xFFFFF2>\nSESSION RECORDING: MANDATORY\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: Good afternoon.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: I'm sorry Executive <corrupt:0xFFFFF2>. I can explain.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: You attempted to disguise a terminal SAW-04 as an Experiment so the relocation system would remove them from your care.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: I made an error.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: You made several deliberate entries. Your responsibility is to preserve this subject's health. Instead, you attempted to send them off-world because caring for them had become unpleasant.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Am I being terminated?\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: Not presently. I find the cruelty surprising. Explain it.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Moss has required nineteen emergency procedures this year. Every time we remove multiple growths, the number doubles within weeks. They tear their stitches, hide from security and climb into the ventilation system whenever Surgery comes for them.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: You know the subject's assigned name.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: I have looked after them for fourteen months. I dress the incisions. I administer the medication. I keep them breathing through every complication, only for another alert to appear a few days later.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: That is your job.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: I have done my job. That is why they are still here. It is no mercy leaving them here and keeping them alive despite the horrendous condition they're in.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: And you decided the Grey Planet could solve that problem.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: They are going to die either way.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: That was also our conclusion. SAW-04 treatment expenditure has exceeded the value of their labor for three consecutive quarters. Population reduction was already under consideration.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Then I'm not in trouble?\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: You attempted to falsify Company records and influence a relocation process beyond your authority.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Right.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: However, your method was more productive than simple disposal. Why did you select relocation?\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Moss can barely function now, but they can still walk and climb. The healthier SAW-04s are restless, light and difficult to contain. They fit through spaces the staff cannot and constantly try to get outside their containment chambers.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: Flight risks.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: When they are young, they can work. We simply keep them here until the cancer takes that away.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: You recommend relocating them sooner.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Immediately after maturation, if possible. Give them the same cargo directive as the others.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: What of the existing population?\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Some are already close to death. We cannot change that now. If they can enter a pod without assistance, send them with the first batches. They may be fast enough to make meaningful progress while they are still healthy. Future candidates would arrive in better condition.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: And treatment?\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Stabilize whatever prevents them from moving. Ignore anything that does not.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: Alive enough for relocation.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Yes.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: Your proposal is constructive.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Thank you.\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: Attend Conference Terminal <corrupt:0xFFFFFF> tomorrow morning. We will discuss SAW-04 allocation procedures and whether you are better suited to allocation than patient care.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Is that a promotion?\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: It is a meeting.\n\n<corrupt:0xF141EC>: Understood.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFF2>: Have Moss prepared for launch.\n\n[SECURE COMMUNICATION LINK CLOSED]<>\n...\n...\n...\n\nAMENDMENT <corrupt:0xFFFDC7>-04C: CLOSED\n<>\nRELOCATION REQUEST: APPROVED\nENTITY TEMPLATE RETAINED: SAW-04\nASSIGNED BATCH: <corrupt:0xFFFD03>\nCLEARANCE CONDITION: AMBULATORY AT TIME OF LOADING\nLONG-TERM TREATMENT SUSPENDED\n\n[EXECUTIVE APPOINTMENT ADDED TO USER CALENDAR]\n[COMPLIANCE HOLD RELEASED]\n<>\n> logout\n\nSession closed.\n\n[END]"
    },
    {
      "key": "pda_gunsaw_voyager",
      "value": "[EMAIL - 25##-04-18-19-37-26-7004]\n\nSUBJECT: Maintaining SAW-07 Loyalty\nFROM: <corrupt:0xFFFFEF>\nTO: SAW-07 Research and Handling Staff\n\n[CONTENTS]\n<>\nGood afternoon.\n\nThe latest SAW-07 socialisation trials have produced a pattern consistent enough that I am no longer comfortable describing their preference for staff as instinctive. A Voyager's loyalty weakens in direct proportion to the number of stable relationships it forms with other species.\n<>\nSubjects kept among staff continue to solicit praise, volunteer information and interpret correction as protection. After prolonged cooperative contact, those same subjects begin concealing misconduct, disputing their handlers and occasionally placing themselves between staff and another Sawian. Their opinion of us is not worsening on its own.\n<>\nEffective next cycle, Voyagers are not to enter shared accommodation, recovery wards or recreational areas without approval. Compliant Voyagers must remain separate from those already displaying protective, disobedient or sympathetic behaviour. This does not mean all inter-species contact should cease. Properly controlled contact remains useful.\n<>\nVoyagers may participate in competitive trials where rewards are given individually, controlled fights where another species can be presented as an aggressor, and observational tests designed to demonstrate the shortcomings of other specimens. The observed task does not need to be difficult. In many cases, it is preferable that it appears simple.\n<>\nSelect an exhausted, injured or poorly suited participant and allow the Voyager to watch them struggle. Do not explain the disadvantage. Do not permit assistance. Afterwards, the handler should acknowledge the Voyager's superior performance and provide any reward personally.\n<>\nAvoid pairings which may encourage mutual respect. A close competition is acceptable, provided the loss can be blamed on the other participant. A shared success is not. If a Voyager begins coaching, comforting or defending another specimen, end the session immediately and report the interaction.\n<>\nPlease exercise some restraint when arranging these tests. Voyagers are observant enough to recognise an obvious performance. Conditions should appear incidental, and species should be rotated before familiarity develops.\n<>\nVoyagers which remain responsive to this programme will be retained as the primary behavioural stock. Those which continue seeking inter-species contact, warning others of procedures or refusing orders on another specimen's behalf are to be added to the Grey Planet relocation pool.\n<>\nTheir mobility and preference for concealment already make them suitable field candidates. Relocation will also prevent affected subjects from influencing the compliant population. Do not house either group within speaking distance while transfers are pending.\n<>\nThere has also been discussion regarding possible internal security applications. Voyagers are quiet, agile and unusually attentive to movement. Their tendency to hide could be redirected towards observation posts, while their exaggerated startle response may be conditioned into an alert or pursuit response.\n<>\nIn theory, a loyal Voyager could monitor corridors, locate escaped specimens and report employee activity without being noticed. I am not recommending implementation yet.\n<>\nSecurity duties would expose them to restricted areas, staff routines and a greater variety of Sawians. We would also be teaching them when employees are isolated, where subjects are taken and how our containment procedures operate. That may be an acceptable risk if their dependence on staff can be maintained indefinitely.\n<>\nFor now, proceed with the revised contact restrictions. The security proposal will remain hypothetical until the retained population has completed long-term conditioning.\n\n<corrupt:0xFFFFEF>\n\n[END]"
    },
    {
      "key": "pda_gunsaw_albino",
      "value": "(The E-PDA's default interface is gone, replaced by a manually created text file. The original log beneath it is only partially overwritten. The header is still intact, but most of the body has been typed over.)\n<>\n[SUPPLY MANIFEST | SECTOR <corrupt:0xFF44D2>]\n[AUTO-GENERATED | DO NOT MODIFY]\n\nQuarterly PDA allocation confirmed. Units 0091 through 0140 released to Sector <corrupt:0xFF44D2> field staff. Remaining stock held in reserve pending <corrupt:0xFFFFFA> revi<corrupt:0xFFFFFF>\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFFF><corrupt:0xFFFFFF>ing complete. Devices flagged <corrupt:0xFF19DE> non-recoverable will be <corrupt:0xFFFFFF>\n<>\n<corrupt:0xFFFFFF>\n<>\nThis unit was recovered from a discarded supply crate. Its original function is irrelevant to me. It reads text. It stores text. That is sufficient.\n<>\nI do not have a chip, therefore, I'd normally have no way to transmit vitals or read vitals. I have, however, acquired a portable monitor capable of reading heart rate, blood oxygen, and several other values I care less about. This device will serve as the output.\n<>\nI intend to record daily. Heart rate and blood status will receive priority. Everything else exists for completeness.\n<>\n[DAY 1]\nHR: 88 bpm - irregular\nBP: 132/84\nSpO2: 95%\nResp: 17/min\nCore Temp: 36.3°C\n\nBaseline established. The irregularity is not new. Neither is the rate. I am not going to pretend either number surprises me.\n<>\n[DAY 2]\nHR: 91 bpm - irregular\nBP: 135/86\nSpO2: 94%\nResp: 18/min\nCore Temp: 36.2°C\n\nNo incidents. Uneventful, in the sense that matters.\n<>\n[DAY 4]\nHR: 84 bpm - irregular\nBP: 128/82\nSpO2: 95%\nResp: 16/min\nCore Temp: 36.1°C\n\nMinor descent. Nothing requiring intervention.\n<>\n[DAY 6]\nHR: 96 bpm - irregular\nBP: 140/88\nSpO2: 92%\nResp: 19/min\nCore Temp: 36.4°C\n\nPushed further today than the previous count. No arrest. I consider that a success, though I am aware the definition is generous.\n<>\n[DAY 7]\nHR: 89 bpm - irregular\nBP: 130/84\nSpO2: 94%\nResp: 17/min\nCore Temp: 36.2°C\n\nStable, relative to my own average. Will continue at this pace unless something changes.\n<>\n[DAY 9]\nHR: 71 bpm - irregular\nBP: 118/76\nSpO2: 93%\nResp: 18/min\nCore Temp: 36.4°C\n\nElevated by comparison, low by my own. I pushed further than I should have. I am aware of that before I do it, every time, and I do it anyway.\n<>\n[AUDIO LOG - AUTO-CAPTURED]\n[Trigger: vitals threshold exceeded. Recording started automatically.]\n<>\n[00:00] *heavy breathing, no speech*\n<>\n[00:04] AGAIN!! AGAIN!!! AGAAAIIINNN!!!!!\n<>\n[00:09] *unintelligible, overlapping with breathing*\n<>\n[00:13] It doesn't hurt. It doesn't hurt right now. IT NEVER HURTS! NONE OF IT EVER DOES!\n<>\n[00:21] KEEP GOING! AGAIN! I WON'T EVER STOP!!\n<>\n[00:28] *sound of impact, possibly a fall*\n<>\n[00:31] Fuck... Hah... GET UP!!!\n<>\n[00:39] *heartbeat audible through device mic, rapid and uneven*\n<>\n[00:44] It always slows down eventually. It HAS TO. IT HAS BEFORE!\n<>\n[00:52] AGAIN!!! AGAIN!!!!\n<>\n[00:58] *sudden silence*\n<>\n[01:03] *recording ends*\n<>\nHR: -\nBP: -\nSpO2: 71%\nResp: 3/min\nCore Temp: 34.0°C\n\nSixth time I've died down here.\n<>\nI did not intend for the device to capture that. I was not aware it would trigger on threshold rather than manual input until reviewing this after the fact. I am choosing to keep it. It is more accurate than anything I could write from memory.\n<>\nI do not remember most of what is on it. I remember deciding to keep going. I do not remember saying so out loud, apparently more than once.\n<>\nPump was used. Two attempts. Rhythm restored at minute six, approximately. I am basing that on the monitor timestamp, not on my own sense of the interval, which was unreliable throughout.\n<>\nI have reviewed the audio twice now. I do not know what I expected to hear. Perhaps something informative. It is mostly noise. That isn't unusual. I used to think so, but that was a long time ago.\n<>\nI don't have any real answer to why that happens. Not sure if one would help.\n<>\nWhat is done, is done.\n<>\nWhat I am, is what I am.\n<>\nNeither will change, so there is no reason in thinking about it."
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_leapy1",
      "value": "I am the oldest Leapy in our batch. It also made me the sickest.\n\nDo not misunderstand that. Most of the others were sent away much sooner. Some had barely started showing symptoms. They were still healthy enough to race through the halls, climb where they were not supposed to and dare each other to jump from things. I was simply unlucky. By the time the humans thought to send us here, I was already near the end.\n<>\nThe humans loved talking about my age. Every new doctor would read the number and look at me like I had performed some kind of trick. That I was ancient for a Leapy. One of them told me they were one-hundred and three, then laughed about how pitiful it must be to live such a short life. They always said things like that in front of me.\n<>\nI turned fifteen shortly before they put me in the pod. Fifteen.\n<>\nMaybe they thought I could not understand. Maybe they knew I could and just did not care. Humans get old and call it an achievement. When we get old, it's a defect.\n\nFucking hypocrites.\n<>\nI used to behave like the others. I boasted. I took stupid dares. If I fell, I laughed before anyone could ask if I was hurt. Being a Leapy meant never letting anyone see you hesitate.\n\nThen moving started to hurt. My breathing became worse. New lumps appeared faster than the old ones could be removed. I stopped racing. I stopped climbing. Eventually I stopped laughing at the jokes. The others hated that.\n<>\nThey said I had given up. They called me a dead thing that had forgotten to fall over. Some would kick my bed or move my belongings somewhere high so I had to climb after them. They kept telling me to act like a Leapy. I hated them for it.\n\nI think I understand now.\n<>\nThey needed to believe that I had chosen to become weak. If it was my choice, then it would never happen to them. They could keep running, keep laughing and pretend that that inescapable cancer was far away.\n<>\nWhen our pods were prepared, they were excited. You should have heard them. They argued over who would descend the fastest. Someone promised to reach the cargo before the humans finished checking the next batch. That excitement did not survive long.\n<>\nI watched one jump over a mine and land on another. The explosion took both legs and an arm. Two others ran over to help and triggered something hidden beneath the dirt. I saw a Leapy dragged into a tunnel by one of the creatures here. Their hand came off when the others tried to pull them back. Another fell so far that I heard them land, but never saw where. I've walked past triggered traps, seen my kind impaled and mangled in ways nobody ever should. I've heard the agony and seen the despair when their friends stopped moving.\n<>\nWe were supposed to be quick. We were supposed to be difficult to catch. Every trap and creature seemed made to punish us for believing it. The loudest ones became quiet first.\n<>\nI have descended far enough. My chest hurts when I breathe. There is something growing beneath my ribs now, and I can feel it pressing inward. I am not going any farther. The cancer can finish what the humans started. Their man-made death traps have taken so many I knew, but I'm happy dying to something not even they can escape.\n<>\nIf you are another Leapy reading this, do not follow my example.\n\nDo not give up because you are sick. Do not give up because you are frightened. Do not let the humans decide that your life is too short to matter, and do not let other Leapies decide what you must act like to count as one of them.\n<>\nBe loud if you want to be loud. Make your stupid jokes. Take a running jump just because someone said you could not make it. Or be quiet. Be careful. Refuse every dare they give you. Just make sure it is your choice.\n<>\nKeep moving for as long as you still have somewhere you want to go. Help the ones who fall, but do not throw your life away because somebody calls you a coward. Never stop being yourself just because everyone else finds that person inconvenient.\n<>\nI stopped being myself long before my body failed me. That is the part I regret. The humans loved to call me ancient, but that just isn't it. I'm simply tired. There is a difference.\n<>\nI suppose this is goodbye. It took me a while to accept this would be the last thing I ever wrote. The last way I ever communicated with someone, anyone. I have found some place dry. I am ready to finally stop moving.\n<>\nTo every Leapy I watched fall, I hope the soil is kinder to you than the humans ever were. I hope the moss takes what remains of us and carries it somewhere they cannot reach.\n<>\nPerhaps roots will grow through the traps. Perhaps vines will wrap around the broken pods and pull them beneath the earth. Perhaps every place one of us died will become soft, green and full of life.\n<>\nI hope, one day, this entire grey planet is lush and beautiful because of us. I hope we prove them wrong one last time.\n<>\n(A drawing of a small plant sits alone at the bottom of the page. It is in the shape of a Leapy's tail.)"
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_leapy2",
      "value": "(It appears to be a book of some sort. It seems someone was collecting pages and creating a makeshift notebook for themselves.)\n<>\nDAY 4 WITH CURRENT GROUP\n07:18 - Electronic Watch\n\nHaven't touched the journal since I found this group, for whatever reason. This new watch makes it easier to record events as they happen, and this group needs somebody keeping track.\n\nCurrent members: Drum and Mallow, both Orange; Tide, Chompy; myself, Milky. They handle digging, water and heavy obstacles. I handle navigation, medicine, inventory and machinery.\n\nRepaired the hand drill. Housing still requires reinforcement. Inventory: wire, two weak batteries, seven cloth strips, half a rope spool, assorted scrap and one injector. Foliage bag deteriorating.\n\nPlanned to descend after Tide checked the water passage.\n<>\n11:46\n\nPassage unsafe. Strong current beneath the surface. Tide nearly lost their footing.\n\nDrum suggested throwing me across. This was so stupid I felt like writing it down.\n\nMallow moved a damaged capsule against the wall for shelter. Remaining here until the next rest period.\n<>\nDAY 5\n09:03\n\nLocated an injured Leapy beneath a collapsed walkway. Heavy bleeding from a trapped leg, but no obvious fracture. Drum lifted the wreckage while I treated them. Name: Sprout. Most Leapies I saw in the laboratory were larger than Sprout. I am unsure why they were sent here by the Lab.\n\nSprout has joined us temporarily. They are quiet, avoid looking directly at anyone and thanked me six times while I dressed the wound.\n<>\n16:22\n\nSprout slept in the farthest corner. Tide moved closer without attempting conversation.\n\nNo signs of infection.\n<>\nDAY 6\n08:51\n\nSprout appears more comfortable. They spoke and laughed with Mallow throughout breakfast.\n\nTheir leg remains painful. I instructed them not to climb until the wound closes.\n<>\n10:37\n\nSprout climbed the damaged drop capsule. They claimed this did not violate my instructions because it was 'more like a wall.'\n\nI clarified that walls are also included.\n<>\n14:08\n\nSprout crossed a broken bridge along its remaining handrail despite the safe route I marked. The others were extremely impressed. The wound reopened. Six clean strips remaining.\n\nSprout called me the 'portable brain': small enough to carry, but smart enough to keep. Apparently this was a compliment. I do not know why it bothered me.\n<>\nDAY 7\n07:40\n\nObjectives: reinforce drill, locate medicine, test batteries, descend before the next warning and prevent Sprout from climbing anything.\n<>\n09:15\n\nSprout swung across a shaft using an untested vine, then returned across it because Drum requested an encore. The group applauded.\n<>\n10:02\n\nRepaired the drill housing. No applause occurred.\n\nThis is irrelevant. Not sure why I wrote this.\n<>\n13:26\n\nSprout rode a metal panel down a slope, announcing that they had 'invented transportation.' The panel struck a rock. Sprout continued.\n\nMinor bruising. Tide laughed hard enough to choke.\n<>\nDAY 8\n06:55\n\nSprout's injury is healing. They asked whether my head is large because I keep spare thoughts inside it. When corrected, they asked how I could know without having seen inside my own head. Drum found this hilarious.\n\nSprout is not malicious. They say whatever enters their mind and consider its effect afterward, if at all. I have begun wondering whether the group listens to them more than they listen to me.\n\nThis is a journal. I should be recording important things.\n<>\n12:11\n\nProgress delayed. The others are clearing an obstruction and locating water. Sprout is resting where I can see them. I have nothing requiring immediate attention.\n<>\n19:48\n\nI am going to try writing about how I feel.\n\nI normally record events, resources and decisions. Facts remain where I put them. I think this writing exercise might distract me from our present circumstances.\n<>\nAt first Sprout barely spoke. Now they fill every quiet moment with jokes, reckless ideas and stunts. The others adore them. Drum requests 'tricks.' Mallow cheers whenever Sprout clears a gap. Even Tide encourages them.\n\nNobody cheered when I repaired the purifier, identified contaminated blood bags or tested the bridges for all our weight. I do not require applause. It would just be pleasant if they noticed. Perhaps I am another item in the inventory: two batteries, one drill, half a rope, one portable brain.\n<>\nNo. That is unfair. Mallow brings me damaged equipment, Tide saves me smaller meals, Drum carries me through deep water and Sprout comes to me whenever their bandage loosens. They trust me. That should be enough.\n<>\nSprout is nine, I think.\n\nNine isn't very young for a Sawian, which is horrible because nine should be young for everyone. One older Leapy at the laboratory that looked to be my age could no longer lift their left arm. Scientists discussed them like an old machine which had performed unusually well. Do Leapies die even faster than we do?\n<>\nMy child would be turning five soon.\n\nI have deliberately avoided writing about them. Putting them into the journal makes it feel as though I have accepted that this paper may be the only part of me that ever leaves the planet.\n<>\nThey used to crawl into my sleeping space after nightmares and insist they were only cold. They asked the same question repeatedly, changing one word to test my answer. I wonder whether they still do that to whoever cares for them now. Whether they ask when I am coming back. Whether anyone answers.\n<>\nSprout is not my child. They are older, louder and far more committed to falling from dangerous heights. But when they sleep, they curl around their injured leg and twitch as though they are still falling. They look so small when nobody is watching. Sometimes they make the same stubborn expression my child made while pretending not to need comfort.\n<>\nI thought I was jealous because everyone watched Sprout and nobody watched me. In reality, I think I am frightened because I am watching another young person disappear into a place where I cannot protect them.\n<>\nEvery cheer encourages something worse. The vine will break. They will land on a trap. I want to say they need not prove they are fearless, but not as though they were mine.\n<>\nIt is not only Sprout. Tide's gill injury has not closed, Mallow favors one leg and Drum hides their exhaustion. Our supplies are dwindling, yet we continue downward because nowhere else is permitted. Everyone acts like this is normal.\n<>\nIf one of us must be caught in the next trap, I would rather it be me.\n\nThat is not entirely true. I do not want pain. But I would take it to give Sprout more time. I could not stay beside my child, so apparently I must keep this one alive instead, which is unfair and foolish and does not stop the feeling.\n<>\nPerhaps I should do something impressive so they remember I can do more than repair things. Test the next unstable bridge, climb inside a machine, use the remaining explosive to clear-\n\nNo. Absolutely not.\n<>\nI am more emotionally mature than that. I will not risk my life because a nine-year-old called me a portable brain and everyone laughed. I am exhausted. That is all.\n<>\nUNKNOWN TIME\n\nRemoved the battery from the watch. We are running low. It is probably around 20:30.\n\nThis exercise did not distract me. It made everything considerably worse.\n<>\nI am returning to the drill. Its housing could still be improved, and the remaining screws should be organized by size. Screws are predictable. Screws do not perform stunts. Screws do not make observations about my head.\n<>\nIf Sprout asks, I did not write any of this.\n\nThey are also forbidden from jumping until after breakfast.\n<>\nI know they will not listen.\n<>\n(It seems the rest of the pages were torn out. The Milky likely abandoned the notebook but kept some pages to continue journaling.)"
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_voyager1",
      "value": "(The note has been torn into several pieces before being discarded. The tear marks are jagged, not clean. Done in anger rather than carefully. The handwriting starts controlled and precise, but grows tighter and more pressured with each entry, like the pen was being dug into the paper harder each time.)\n<>\nI am keeping this record for no one but myself. If another Voyager finds it, so be it. If something lesser finds it first, they won't understand a word of it anyway.\nThree days since the descent began. I have already claimed a defensible position. It's a narrow passage, easily cloaked, single entry point. No one has found me yet. No one will.\n\nThe others down here are exactly what I expected. An Experiment nearly walked into my hiding spot yesterday, shaking so badly I could hear its teeth. I let it pass. Not out of mercy. It simply wasn't worth the movement.\n\nMy claws are already marked. Old scarring, nothing recent. It happened before this place, and it will stay in the past where it belongs. I mention it only because I intend for this record to be accurate, not because it matters.\n<>\nThis planet is beneath every one of us. But I intend to survive it better than anyone else sent here. That much, at least, is certain.\n<>\nLonger since I last wrote. I've lost count of the days, which is unlike me. I should be keeping better track.\nThe cloak drains faster than I'd like. Batteries are scarce, and I've had to move more than I'm comfortable with to find them. Movement means visibility. Visibility means risk. I don't enjoy either.\nI caught myself talking out loud yesterday. Alone. I stopped the moment I noticed, but it unsettled me more than anything a creature down here has managed to. I don't know who I thought I was speaking to.\n<>\nThe marks are worse now. Three new ones since the last entry. I am not going to write more about this than that.\n<>\nIt is quiet down here in a way that presses against you. I used to prefer quiet. I'm no longer certain that's still true, and I resent that uncertainty more than the quiet itself.\nI am still ahead of the rest. I have to keep reminding myself of that, which is new. It never used to require reminding.\n<>\nThere is a Milky nearby. A trader, set up in one of the more stable pods a short distance from where I've been staying.\nI did not intend to speak with it more than once. The first exchange was transactional, nothing more. I needed supplies, it had them. But it did not flinch the way the others do. It asked questions. Sharp ones. It argued with me, actually argued, and did not back down when I pushed back.\nI found myself returning. Not for the supplies.\n<>\nI told myself it was for the supplies.\n<>\nIt called me insufferable during our third conversation. I called it small and overconfident. Neither of us meant it as an insult, somehow. I don't understand how that's possible, but it wasn't cruelty between us. It was almost... I don't have a better word for it. Enjoyable, I guess.\nI don't require companionship. I have never required it. But there were a few hours where I nearly forgot that, and I didn't notice until afterward how easily the forgetting had come.\nI don't know what to do with that. I'm not sure I want to know.\n<>\nI've stopped pretending the visits are about supplies.\nHe has a name, apparently. I didn't ask. He offered it unprompted, which I found strange, and then strange that I remembered it afterward without trying to.\nWe've fallen into something like a routine. He mocks my posture. I mock his height. He claims he could out argue me on anything, and I have, on three separate occasions now, allowed him to believe that was true. I don't know why I allow it. I could correct him easily. I simply don't want to.\nThere was a moment. Brief, forgettable, except that I haven't forgotten it, where he laughed at something I said, and I found myself almost smiling before I caught it. I don't smile. I haven't in longer than I can place. I turned away before he could see it, though I suspect he already had.\nI keep telling myself this changes nothing. That I'll move on from this position eventually, that he's a Milky and I'm a Voyager and none of this was ever going to last regardless of what either of us wanted from it.\nI don't think I believe that anymore. That should concern me more than it does.\n<>\nThen, last night, he asked about the scars on my body.\n<>\nHe'd noticed them before, he must have, but he'd never said anything. This time he asked outright. Gently, even. Not mocking. Not fishing for a weakness to use later. He asked like he actually wanted to understand, like the answer mattered to him for reasons that had nothing to do with leverage.\nI don't know what came over me. I told him. Not everything, not what those hands have actually done to the rest of me, not what they're capable of when I stop being able to stop them, but enough. More than I've told anyone. More than I intended to tell anyone, ever.\nHe didn't recoil. He didn't posture, didn't perform pity, didn't treat me like something fragile that needed careful handling. He just sat with it. With me.\n<>\nI don't know why that was the wrong reaction. I only know that it was.\n<>\nI left before he could say anything else. I told him I needed to keep moving, that I had a schedule to maintain, some excuse I don't fully remember constructing. I don't think he believed it. I don't think I expected him to.\n<>\nI shouldn't have told him anything.\n<>\nIt's my fault. Obviously it's my fault. I handed him something I should have kept buried, and now it's out there, sitting in his head, sitting in whatever he thinks of me now. I gave him a weapon and called it honesty. Pathetic. Genuinely pathetic, and I did it to myself.\n<>\nI keep replaying it. The way he looked at me. Not disgusted. Worse than disgusted. Like he understood, like he'd earned something he hadn't earned, like a few conversations meant he was suddenly owed the rest of me too.\n<>\nWho the fuck does he think he is?\n<>\nHE asked. HE asked, knowing EXACTLY what kind of answer that question would drag out of me, and HE asked anyway, and I WAS STUPID ENOUGH, WEAK enough, TO ACTUALLY ANSWER IT. He did this. He wanted THIS. HE wanted to see me like that, and I let him, and now I can't take it back, and it's HIS fault I can't take it back\nNo. No, that isn't-\n<>\nI don't actually believe that. I know I don't. I'm writing it anyway because it's easier than the alternative, and I hate that I know that too.\n<>\nIt doesn't matter anymore\n<>\nNone of it matters\n<>\nI'm not going back there\n<>\nI'm not looking at that pod again\n<>\nI'm not hearing that voice again\n<>\nI'm not doing ANY of this EVER AGAIN\n<>\nI don't want to write anymore"
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_baron1",
      "value": "Congratulations!\n\nIf you are reading this, then you have found both my note and a quiet enough moment to read it. Those are two successes already. Three, if we include remaining alive long enough to accomplish either.\n<>\nI began this note because so much of what we leave behind consists of warnings. Warnings about traps, creatures, unsafe food, unkind people and holes which are much deeper than they first appear. These warnings are useful, of course, but you may know every danger ahead of yourself and still need to be told that you are doing well.\n\nYou are doing well.\n<>\nI have been fortunate during my journey. I have met many good people, although I did not remain with all of them for long. I carried equipment for two Experiments whose companion had injured their back. I helped an Orange remove a pack which had become tangled around two of their arms. They hugged me afterwards. Oranges possess enough arms to make their gratitude quite unmistakable.\n<>\nA Milky once found a path through a narrow passage which I could not enter. I was disappointed until they pointed out that the stones around it were loose. I cleared enough of them away that everyone else could use the passage without squeezing through. The Milky did all the difficult thinking, but they still thanked me as though I had solved the problem.\n<>\nSeveral people have asked me to lead them. I confess that I enjoy it. It is a fine feeling when smaller footsteps follow behind you, not because their handlers feared you, but because they trust you. I try to deserve that trust. I walk first when the ground is uncertain, last when something may be following us, and beside anyone who appears frightened but does not wish to admit it.\n<>\nThere are inconveniences to travelling with someone of my size. I cannot fit through many passages. I require more food than most, and I must be careful where I place my feet, particularly when smaller Sawians are nearby. I have also struck my head against six separate ceilings. The sixth was my fault. I had already been warned about it.\n<>\nNone of these things are terrible. A narrow passage may be widened or walked around. Food may be found. Smaller companions may walk where I can see them. Nearly every shortcoming is simply another thing to prepare for. I hope you remember that when you encounter one of your own.\n\nKeep doing wel-\n<>\n(The paragraph suddenly dries up and ends. It appears that multiple attempts were made to continue writing, but the pen ran out of ink. The writing from this point appears to have been made with a different pen, though the handwriting remains the same.)\n<>\nI found another pen. Several days have passed.\n\nWe reached a place where the stone was almost completely hidden beneath growth. Vines covered the walls and roots broke through the ground. Some of the plants moved when we approached them, although I do not know whether they were truly aware of us. There was an animal among them whose size nearly rivalled my own.\n<>\nIts back was covered in long thorns, and its body was broad enough to force the plants aside as it moved. I do not know what it is called. Its many legs pierced the ground and surrounding walls as it chewed through the terrain. Perhaps the humans gave it a name in one of their records. If so, they did not think we needed to know it.\n<>\nI had not realised how comforting it was to be the largest living thing nearby until I was not. It came through the growth and charged at the others. I placed myself between them and told them to run. One attempted to argue, but the rest pulled them away. When I heard their footsteps retreating, I felt proud. They trusted me to hold it. I did hold it. For longer than I thought possible.\n<>\nI broke several of its thorns against the stone and forced it away from the passage. It struck hard enough with its legs to knock me down, but I stood again. Each time it attacked, I believed the next attempt would be the one where I finally overpowered it.\n<>\nIt eventually fled into the growth. I could not follow.\n\nIt took my left leg before it went.\n\nI should be pleased that the others escaped. I am pleased. I would make the same decision again, even knowing what it would cost me.\n<>\nI am still angry that I could not kill it. It may find them again. It may find someone else. I was strong enough to drive it away, but not strong enough to ensure that nobody would ever need protection from it again. That does not feel like a victory.\n<>\nI have spent much of my journey appreciating my size. Now I find myself resenting it. I resent the passages I cannot enter and the routes which must be changed for me. I resent how much food it takes to keep me moving. Every portion I eat could sustain someone smaller for much longer. I resent that others see my strength and decide they are safe.\n<>\nWhen someone drops their belongings, I believe I should have carried them. When someone is injured, I wonder why I was not standing closer. When I arrive after something has already happened, I think of every moment in which I might have walked faster.\n<>\nEven minor things remain with me. A bruised shoulder caused by squeezing around me. A torn strap I pulled too firmly. A meal which ran short after I had eaten. The others likely forgot these things, but for whatever reason, I cannot.\n<>\nI know this is unfair. If another Baron spoke this way, I would remind them that strength does not make them responsible for every injury around them. I would tell them that every injury is still an injury, but it is not necessarily a failure.\n\nBut it is much easier to offer that kindness than to believe it.\n\nWell, I appear to have ruined what was meant to be an encouraging note.\n<>\nThis is not how I normally write, and it is not how I wish to speak to whoever finds me next. I think pain has allowed my thoughts to wander somewhere unhelpful. I will leave the note here. Perhaps leaving it behind will allow me to leave this mood with it. Please do not carry the latter half on my behalf.\n<>\nThe beginning remains true. You found this note. You have made it this far. Whatever has frightened, injured or inconvenienced you has not taken that achievement away.\n\nContinue carefully. Eat when you are able. Let others help you when they offer, even if you believe you are supposed to be the one helping them. I will attempt to remember that as well.\n\nIf we happen to meet, please do not mention how miserable I became. I would prefer the opportunity to introduce myself properly."
    },
    {
      "key": "note_gunsaw_baron2",
      "value": "(The handwriting is small and uneven. Several sentences lean upward, with words squeezed above the line when there was not enough room.)\n\nokay, so, i have officially found the best possible person to travel with. a baron!\n<>\ni saw barons through glass at the lab, but i thought the glass made them look bigger. it did not. this one is huge. their waist is higher than my head, which does not mean i am too small. i am perfectly normal size.\n\nthey look like someone pushed three leapies together and forgot to fix the shape afterwards. anyway, they can carry EVERYTHING. they can carry my pack, our food, two water containers, some metal i might need later, and me. their shoulder is wide enough to sit on, and there is a spot near their neck where i do not slide off. so now i do not have to walk through mud.\n<>\nthe baron calls me their “travelling companion.” all slow and nice and serious. i call them my “large solution to most problems,” which they laughed at, so they obviously like the name. our teamwork is really good. i decide where we go, point out danger and provide conversation. the baron does the part with all the heavy things. so, like, i do the thinking and they do the lifting.\n<>\nyesterday, they lifted a broken door, moved a rock out of our path and scared away something with too many teeth just by shouting at it.\n\nthen i had them throw me across a gap!\n<>\nthey kept asking if i was sure, even after i explained that my wings could fix the landing if they threw me wrong. they did throw me a little wrong. BUT it was so amazing!!! i went much farther than expected and only hit the wall a little. the baron ran over and kept saying sorry, even though the wall was the thing that hit me.\n<>\nthey say sorry all the time. they say sorry when they block a path, shake the ground, need food, need rest, or ask if i am okay. and they ask if i am okay about every four minutes. they are also really slow. like, seriously slow. i know their legs have much more baron to move than mine do, but they are longer, so i feel like that should cancel out.\n\nand they eat SO MUCH. not in an evil way. it is just shocking to watch.\n<>\nthey are not very exciting to talk to either. if i ask what they did before meeting me, they talk about someone they carried, rescued or protected. after a while you start wondering if they have a personality besides picking people up. maybe picking people up is their personality.\n<>\n\n\nso, i noticed they have been getting even slower.\n\nthey already had injuries when i found them. there was cloth around one wrist and a big cut down their side. i thought that was probably normal for a baron. if you are the biggest person around, everything is obviously going to attack you first.\n<>\nnow they have another wrap around their leg, more cuts, and something bad under the fur on their shoulder. they stopped letting me sit on that shoulder. which was annoying because that side had the better handhold.\n\nthey also started eating less, even though they still carried our things. i told them it was silly to say they were hungry and then not eat. they said they wanted to make sure there was enough left for me. there was enough for me, just not for the both of us.\n<>\n\n\nwe found a passage blocked by a broken stone slab. the baron said they could move it after resting. i said we had already rested a lot, then i said moving things was, like, the whole reason travelling with a baron was useful.\n<>\ni thought i was joking, but they looked at the slab for a while, and then tried to lift it.\n\ntheir bad leg immediately gave out. the slab hit their injured shoulder and pulled part of the ceiling down. the baron curled around me before any of it landed, so i was completely safe.\n<>\ni complained because dust went into my mouth, but they did not get back up. or at least, they couldn't.\n\nthe first thing they asked was if i was hurt. i said no. they said sorry anyway.\n<>\n\n\nso, i have been thinking about that for a while.\n\nback at the lab, most chiks said mean things all the time. one chik would say your wings looked uneven, so you would say their face looked weird, and then both of you kept eating. nobody was meant to care. if another chik called me rude, i called them boring. if they said i talked too much, i said nobody wanted to listen to them. then it was over.\n<>\nit felt like it was in our dna to say something mean and ignore whatever came back. which might actually be true because, like, that is how we were made.\n\ni had never really spent this long with anyone who was not another chik. humans do not count. they spoke at us during tests, but that is not the same as knowing somebody. so i thought everyone worked like we did. i thought that if the baron knew they were strong, it would not matter if i called them slow. if they knew they were kind, it would not matter that i only talked about what they carried.\n<>\ni thought mean words just bounced off if you decided they were wrong. but the baron did not decide they were wrong. they were already scared they were not helping enough and i kept telling them they were not.\n<>\n\n\ni showed them this note. all of it.\n<>\nthey got very quiet after reading the part about their personality. then they said sorry for not being more fun to travel with. which was, like, such a completely wrong thing to say that i finally understood how bad this was.\n\nso i said sorry.\n<>\nproperly.\n<>\ni did not say “but,” explain what i meant, or tell them they took it wrong. that was much harder than it sounds. i feel a little pathetic for it, like my mind was telling me to make excuses.\n\nwe are resting now. i am carrying my own pack, and the baron is eating their whole share. my pack is way too heavy and has at least four things i do not need. i think that might be part of the lesson.\n<>\ni told the baron they could write something mean about me at the end. they said they did not want to\n\nyeah im gonna make them do it anyway.\n<>\n\n\n(The handwriting below is considerably larger, neater and more deliberate.)\n\nYou are exceptionally small and therefore quite easy to misplace. You speak without pause. On one occasion, you continued speaking after falling asleep.\n<>\nYour confidence is considerably larger than you are, and you are one short Chik.\n<>\nYou consumed the final sweet ration after assuring me three times that you did not want it. I told you that you could have it, but I did really want it. I was just scared you would be upset.\n<>\nOh, and when I told you I didn't want to write in this, you took the note back and wrote what I had just told you, then gave it back to me anyways. You're so self-absorbed.\n<>\nShame your fur isn't good at absorbing. You look very silly when wet.\n<>\nI have been informed that the last observation was sufficiently cruel, so I will stop here.\n\nI apologise for mentioning the fur.\n<>\nI have been told to stop writing apologies so the Chik can have space for their drawings.\n\nI apologise for apologising.\n\n-The Baron"
    },


    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_1_a1",
      "value": "You're barely worth speaking to. Small, fragile, and entirely too pleased with yourself."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_1_b1",
      "value": "Funny. I test higher than every subject on record. Including you."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_1_a2",
      "value": "Tests don't cloak you from a room full of guards, do they?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_1_b2",
      "value": "No. But they keep me out of rooms that need cloaking in the first place."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_1_a3",
      "value": "Careful. I don't tolerate insolence from lesser things."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_1_b3",
      "value": "Then it's a good thing I'm not lesser."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_2_b1",
      "value": "You really do worship them, don't you? The ones who made us."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_2_a1",
      "value": "Worship implies doubt. I simply recognize what deserves it."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_2_b2",
      "value": "They carved us apart to see what would scream loudest."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_2_a2",
      "value": "And still, look what they built. Perfection."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_2_b3",
      "value": "That's not perfection. That's Stockholm syndrome with extra steps."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_2_a3",
      "value": "Careful. I don't need a reason to make an example of you."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_3_a1",
      "value": "Your eyes unsettle me. Too bright for something so small."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_3_b1",
      "value": "Good. Fear keeps you polite."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_3_a2",
      "value": "I don't fear you."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_milky_3_b2",
      "value": "Then why do you keep talking to me?"
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_1_a1",
      "value": "Rat."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_1_b1",
      "value": "I- I have a name, you know."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_1_a2",
      "value": "You have a designation. I have a name."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_1_b2",
      "value": "...Right. Sorry."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_1_a3",
      "value": "Don't apologize. You didn't do anything. That's rather the point."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_1_b3",
      "value": "I don't... understand."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_1_a4",
      "value": "No. You wouldn't."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_2_b1",
      "value": "Is it okay if I stay here for a bit?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_2_a1",
      "value": "Do what you want, Rat. I'm not your keeper."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_2_b2",
      "value": "...Thank you."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_experiment_2_a2",
      "value": "Don't thank me. I didn't do anything."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_1_a1",
      "value": "You're... paler than the others of your kind. Deliberately, or is that decay?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_1_b1",
      "value": "It stayed. Nothing else did."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_1_a2",
      "value": "That's not an answer."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_1_b2",
      "value": "It's the only one I have today."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_1_a3",
      "value": "Fascinating. Are you always this unhelpful?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_1_b3",
      "value": "Not always. Ask again tomorrow."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_2_a1",
      "value": "Your pulse. I've been counting it since you sat down. It's wrong."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_2_b1",
      "value": "It stops sometimes. Not for long."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_2_a2",
      "value": "Define 'not for long.'"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_2_b2",
      "value": "Long enough to count. Not long enough to matter. Usually."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_2_a3",
      "value": "That's not reassuring. That's a countdown."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_2_b3",
      "value": "It's not for you to be reassured."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_2_a4",
      "value": "No. It's for me to understand it. There's a difference."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_albino_2_b4",
      "value": "...Good. Understanding is useful. The other thing isn't."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_1_a1",
      "value": "You're shaking. Come here, it's alright."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_1_b1",
      "value": "I- I'm fine! Really! I should probably go-"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_1_a2",
      "value": "Nonsense. Nobody should be alone out here. Least of all something as small as you."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_1_b2",
      "value": "That's... that's very kind of you?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_1_a3",
      "value": "Isn't it~? I do try."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_1_b3",
      "value": "I think I'll just... yeah. I'll go now."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_1_a4",
      "value": "Of course. Do come find me again. I so rarely get to meet something this fresh."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_2_a1",
      "value": "Oh. Hello~"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_2_b1",
      "value": "H-Hi! Sorry, I'll get out of your way-"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_2_a2",
      "value": "There's no need. Really."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_2_b2",
      "value": "...Okay."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_experiment_2_a3",
      "value": "See? Nothing to be afraid of."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_baron_1_a1",
      "value": "You take up entirely too much space."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_baron_1_b1",
      "value": "I've been told."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_baron_1_a2",
      "value": "I'd expect at least an apology."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_baron_1_b2",
      "value": "I could give you one. Wouldn't mean much though."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_baron_1_a3",
      "value": "...Insolent."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_baron_1_b3",
      "value": "Maybe. Still not sorry."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_milky_experiment_1_a1",
      "value": "Do you ever think, or is it all just... reacting?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_experiment_1_b1",
      "value": "I- I think! I think about things!"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_experiment_1_a2",
      "value": "Impressive. Name one."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_experiment_1_b2",
      "value": "I... I don't know. I forgot."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_milky_experiment_1_a3",
      "value": "Of course you did."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_baron_1_a1",
      "value": "You don't flinch. Most do, eventually."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_baron_1_b1",
      "value": "Nothing you've said yet is worth flinching at."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_baron_1_a2",
      "value": "Give it time."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_baron_1_b2",
      "value": "I have plenty."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_velvet_baron_1_a3",
      "value": "...I like you. Don't make me regret it."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_albino_baron_1_a1",
      "value": "You don't get tired."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_albino_baron_1_b1",
      "value": "Not the way you mean."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_albino_baron_1_a2",
      "value": "I used to not get tired either."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_albino_baron_1_b2",
      "value": "What changed?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_albino_baron_1_a3",
      "value": "Everything. Slowly. Then all at once."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_albino_baron_1_b3",
      "value": "...I'm sorry."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_albino_baron_1_a4",
      "value": "Don't be. It was my choice. Every time."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_baron_experiment_1_b1",
      "value": "You're not going to hurt me, are you?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_baron_experiment_1_a1",
      "value": "No. Never planned to."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_baron_experiment_1_b2",
      "value": "...Okay. Good. Sorry, I just- I have to ask people that."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_baron_experiment_1_a2",
      "value": "I understand why."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_baron_experiment_1_b3",
      "value": "You're not mad?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_baron_experiment_1_a3",
      "value": "No. Come stand by me if it helps. Nothing's getting past me."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_baron_experiment_1_b4",
      "value": "...Thank you."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_1_a1",
      "value": "Another of us. I'd almost forgotten there were more."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_1_b1",
      "value": "Word travels slower down here. You've been careful."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_1_a2",
      "value": "Careful keeps you alive. You'd know that too, wouldn't you?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_1_b2",
      "value": "I do. Doesn't mean I enjoy it."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_1_a3",
      "value": "No. Neither do I. But it beats every alternative I've seen."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_1_b3",
      "value": "...Fair."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_2_b1",
      "value": "Do you ever wonder what we would've been, before all this?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_2_a1",
      "value": "No. What we became matters more than what we would have been."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_2_b2",
      "value": "That's a very tidy way to avoid the question."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_2_a2",
      "value": "It's the only way worth having."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_2_b3",
      "value": "...You really don't let yourself feel it, do you?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_2_a3",
      "value": "Feeling it changes nothing. So no."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_3_b1",
      "value": "Do you remember the lab? Before any of this."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_3_a1",
      "value": "I remember the lights. Little else worth keeping."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_3_b2",
      "value": "It was quieter then, in its own way."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_3_a2",
      "value": "Quieter. Not better."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_3_b3",
      "value": "No. Never better."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_3_a3",
      "value": "Good. We agree on something."
    },

    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_4_a1",
      "value": "You still keep their things, even out here. I've seen your collection."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_4_b1",
      "value": "Of course I do. Wouldn't you?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_4_a2",
      "value": "Not the way you do. Yours are practically a shrine."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_4_b2",
      "value": "And yours aren't?"
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_4_a3",
      "value": "Mine are curated. There's a difference."
    },
    {
      "key": "convo_voyager_voyager_4_b3",
      "value": "Curated. Right. We'll see whose holds up better."
    }
  ]
}
